nswer.type/math "4+2ri"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3c080a6ff42aca4bb5dbba2287e3fb4ebe009721ba3a2f1ef790fd5c0bca930"} {"question" "q-281297" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A quadratic equation with real coefficients has a root ${x}_{1}=2p-3q\\text{i}.$ What is the other root? **Use $i$ to denote the imaginary unit.**\n\n${x}_{2}=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3c080a6ff42aca4bb5dbba2287e3fb4ebe009721ba3a2f1ef790fd5c0bca930"} {"question" "q-281297" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given that the quadratic equation has real coefficients and that ${x}_{1}=2p-3q\\text{i}$ is a root. Therefore, its complex conjugate ${x}_{2}=2p+3q\\text{i}$ is also a root." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3c080a6ff42aca4bb5dbba2287e3fb4ebe009721ba3a2f1ef790fd5c0bca930"} {"question" "q-281297" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6abedbd615512b719ea8cf60c57c1be5709c60e61b06e8ccc841cea1a2337307"} {"question" "q-281297" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3c080a6ff42aca4bb5dbba2287e3fb4ebe009721ba3a2f1ef790fd5c0bca930"} {"question" "q-281297" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2p+3qi"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3c080a6ff42aca4bb5dbba2287e3fb4ebe009721ba3a2f1ef790fd5c0bca930"} {"question" "q-28502" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A quadratic equation with real coefficients has a root $x=3-\\text{i}.$ What is the other root?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3c080a6ff42aca4bb5dbba2287e3fb4ebe009721ba3a2f1ef790fd5c0bca930"} {"question" "q-28502" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given that the quadratic equation has real coefficients and that $x=3-\\text{i}$ is a root. Therefore, its complex conjugate $x=3+\\text{i}$ is also a root." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3c080a6ff42aca4bb5dbba2287e3fb4ebe009721ba3a2f1ef790fd5c0bca930"} {"question" "q-28502" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6abedbd615512b719ea8cf60c57c1be5709c60e61b06e8ccc841cea1a2337307"} {"question" "q-28502" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3c080a6ff42aca4bb5dbba2287e3fb4ebe009721ba3a2f1ef790fd5c0bca930"} {"question" "q-28502" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-3+\\text{i}"] ["math" "1+3\\text{i}"] ["math" "1-3\\text{i}"] ["math" "3+\\text{i}"] ["math" "\\text{i}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3c080a6ff42aca4bb5dbba2287e3fb4ebe009721ba3a2f1ef790fd5c0bca930"} {"question" "q-28502" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3+\\text{i}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3c080a6ff42aca4bb5dbba2287e3fb4ebe009721ba3a2f1ef790fd5c0bca930"} {"question" "q-83584" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}}{2}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $6$, find the equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3c080a6ff42aca4bb5dbba2287e3fb4ebe009721ba3a2f1ef790fd5c0bca930"} {"question" "q-83584" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and  $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}}{2}$ is a root, its conjugate  $x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}}{2}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}}{2})(x+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}}{2})=0\n$$\n\nMultiplying out the parentheses and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}}{2})}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\frac{2}{4}(-1) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}}{2},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $6.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $6.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}) & =0 \\\\ 6{x}^{2}+3 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3c080a6ff42aca4bb5dbba2287e3fb4ebe009721ba3a2f1ef790fd5c0bca930"} {"question" "q-83584" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6abedbd615512b719ea8cf60c57c1be5709c60e61b06e8ccc841cea1a2337307"} {"question" "q-83584" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3c080a6ff42aca4bb5dbba2287e3fb4ebe009721ba3a2f1ef790fd5c0bca930"} {"question" "q-83584" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "6{x}^{2}+2=0"] ["math" "6{x}^{2}+3=0"] ["math" "6{x}^{2}+6=0"] ["math" "6{x}^{2}-2=0"] ["math" "6{x}^{2}-3=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3c080a6ff42aca4bb5dbba2287e3fb4ebe009721ba3a2f1ef790fd5c0bca930"} {"question" "q-83584" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6{x}^{2}+3=0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3c080a6ff42aca4bb5dbba2287e3fb4ebe009721ba3a2f1ef790fd5c0bca930"} {"question" "q-28523" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=2\\text{i}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $3$, find the equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3c080a6ff42aca4bb5dbba2287e3fb4ebe009721ba3a2f1ef790fd5c0bca930"} {"question" "q-28523" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and  $x=2\\text{i}$ is a root, its conjugate  $x=-2\\text{i}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-2\\text{i})(x+2\\text{i})=0\n$$\n\nMultiplying out the parentheses and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-(2\\text{i})^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4(-1) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+4 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=\\pm 2\\text{i},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $3.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $3.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3({x}^{2}+4) & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+12 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3c080a6ff42aca4bb5dbba2287e3fb4ebe009721ba3a2f1ef790fd5c0bca930"} {"question" "q-28523" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6abedbd615512b719ea8cf60c57c1be5709c60e61b06e8ccc841cea1a2337307"} {"question" "q-28523" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3c080a6ff42aca4bb5dbba2287e3fb4ebe009721ba3a2f1ef790fd5c0bca930"} {"question" "q-28523" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3{x}^{2}+12=0"] ["math" "3{x}^{2}+4=0"] ["math" "3{x}^{2}+6=0"] ["math" "3{x}^{2}-12=0"] ["math" "3{x}^{2}-4=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3c080a6ff42aca4bb5dbba2287e3fb4ebe009721ba3a2f1ef790fd5c0bca930"} {"question" "q-28523" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3{x}^{2}+12=0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3c080a6ff42aca4bb5dbba2287e3fb4ebe009721ba3a2f1ef790fd5c0bca930"} {"question" "q-160005" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}\\text{ }\\,\\text{i}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $4$, find the equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3c080a6ff42aca4bb5dbba2287e3fb4ebe009721ba3a2f1ef790fd5c0bca930"} {"question" "q-160005" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and  $x=\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}\\text{ }\\,\\text{i}$ is a root, its conjugate  $x=-\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}\\text{ }\\,\\text{i}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:  \n\n$$\n(x-\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}\\text{ }\\,\\text{i})(x+\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}\\text{ }\\,\\text{i})=0\n$$\n\n   \n\nMultiplying out the parentheses and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{(\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}\\text{ }\\,\\text{i})}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\frac{3}{2}(-1) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}\\text{ }\\,\\text{i},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $4.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $4.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}) & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+6 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3c080a6ff42aca4bb5dbba2287e3fb4ebe009721ba3a2f1ef790fd5c0bca930"} {"question" "q-160005" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6abedbd615512b719ea8cf60c57c1be5709c60e61b06e8ccc841cea1a2337307"} {"question" "q-160005" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3c080a6ff42aca4bb5dbba2287e3fb4ebe009721ba3a2f1ef790fd5c0bca930"} {"question" "q-160005" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "4{x}^{2}+2=0"] ["math" "4{x}^{2}+4=0"] ["math" "4{x}^{2}+6=0"] ["math" "4{x}^{2}-4=0"] ["math" "4{x}^{2}-6=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3c080a6ff42aca4bb5dbba2287e3fb4ebe009721ba3a2f1ef790fd5c0bca930"} {"question" "q-160005" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4{x}^{2}+6=0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3c080a6ff42aca4bb5dbba2287e3fb4ebe009721ba3a2f1ef790fd5c0bca930"} {"question" "q-281325" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=\\frac{7\\text{i}}{5}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $25,$ find the equation. Write your answer in the form $a{x}^{2}+bx+c=0,$ where $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ are real constants.\n\n{{field-1}}$=0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3c080a6ff42aca4bb5dbba2287e3fb4ebe009721ba3a2f1ef790fd5c0bca930"} {"question" "q-281325" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and $x=\\frac{7\\text{i}}{5}$ is a root, its conjugate $x=-\\frac{7\\text{i}}{5}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-\\frac{7\\text{i}}{5})(x+\\frac{7\\text{i}}{5})=0\n$$\n\nMultiplying out the parentheses and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{(\\frac{7\\text{i}}{5})}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\frac{49}{25}(-1) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+\\frac{49}{25} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=\\pm \\frac{7\\text{i}}{5},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $25.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $25.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25({x}^{2}+\\frac{49}{25}) & =0 \\\\ 25{x}^{2}+49 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3c080a6ff42aca4bb5dbba2287e3fb4ebe009721ba3a2f1ef790fd5c0bca930"} {"question" "q-281325" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6abedbd615512b719ea8cf60c57c1be5709c60e61b06e8ccc841cea1a2337307"} {"question" "q-281325" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3c080a6ff42aca4bb5dbba2287e3fb4ebe009721ba3a2f1ef790fd5c0bca930"} {"question" "q-281325" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "25x^2+49"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3c080a6ff42aca4bb5dbba2287e3fb4ebe009721ba3a2f1ef790fd5c0bca930"} {"question" "q-281332" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}}{5}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $25,$ find the equation. Write your answer in the form $a{x}^{2}+bx+c=0,$ where $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ are real constants.\n\n{{field-1}}$=0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3c080a6ff42aca4bb5dbba2287e3fb4ebe009721ba3a2f1ef790fd5c0bca930"} {"question" "q-281332" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}}{5}$ is a root, its conjugate $x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}}{5}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}}{5})(x+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}}{5})=0\n$$\n\nMultiplying out the parentheses and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}}{5})}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\frac{2}{25}(-1) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+\\frac{2}{25} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}}{5},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $25.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $25.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25({x}^{2}+\\frac{2}{25}) & =0 \\\\ 25{x}^{2}+2 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3c080a6ff42aca4bb5dbba2287e3fb4ebe009721ba3a2f1ef790fd5c0bca930"} {"question" "q-281332" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6abedbd615512b719ea8cf60c57c1be5709c60e61b06e8ccc841cea1a2337307"} {"question" "q-281332" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3c080a6ff42aca4bb5dbba2287e3fb4ebe009721ba3a2f1ef790fd5c0bca930"} {"question" "q-281332" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "25x^2+2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3c080a6ff42aca4bb5dbba2287e3fb4ebe009721ba3a2f1ef790fd5c0bca930"} {"question" "q-28224" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=-3-2\\text{i}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $5$, find the equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3c080a6ff42aca4bb5dbba2287e3fb4ebe009721ba3a2f1ef790fd5c0bca930"} {"question" "q-28224" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and $x=-3-2\\text{i}$ is a root, its conjugate $x=-3+2\\text{i}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-(-3-2\\text{i}))(x-(-3+2\\text{i}))=0\n$$\n\nBy expanding the parentheses, we can write the equation in standard form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-x(-3+2\\text{i})-x(-3-2\\text{i})+(-3-2\\text{i})(-3+2\\text{i}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+3x-2x\\text{i}+3x+2x\\text{i}+9-4{\\text{i}}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+6x+9+4 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+6x+13 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=-3\\pm 2\\text{i},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $5.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $5.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5({x}^{2}+6x+13) & =0 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+30x+65 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3c080a6ff42aca4bb5dbba2287e3fb4ebe009721ba3a2f1ef790fd5c0bca930"} {"question" "q-28224" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6abedbd615512b719ea8cf60c57c1be5709c60e61b06e8ccc841cea1a2337307"} {"question" "q-28224" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3c080a6ff42aca4bb5dbba2287e3fb4ebe009721ba3a2f1ef790fd5c0bca930"} {"question" "q-28224" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "5{x}^{2}+30x+13=0"] ["math" "5{x}^{2}+30x+65=0"] ["math" "5{x}^{2}+65x+30=0"] ["math" "5{x}^{2}+6x+65=0"] ["math" "5{x}^{2}-30x-65=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3c080a6ff42aca4bb5dbba2287e3fb4ebe009721ba3a2f1ef790fd5c0bca930"} {"question" "q-28224" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5{x}^{2}+30x+65=0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3c080a6ff42aca4bb5dbba2287e3fb4ebe009721ba3a2f1ef790fd5c0bca930"} {"question" "q-28215" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=-1+\\text{i}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $1,$ find the equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3c080a6ff42aca4bb5dbba2287e3fb4ebe009721ba3a2f1ef790fd5c0bca930"} {"question" "q-28215" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and $x=-1+\\text{i}$ is a root, its conjugate $x=-1-\\text{i}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-(-1+\\text{i}))(x-(-1-\\text{i}))=0\n$$\n\nBy expanding the parentheses, we can write the equation in standard form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-x(-1+\\text{i})-x(-1-\\text{i})+(-1+\\text{i})(-1-\\text{i}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+x-x\\text{i}+x+x\\text{i}+1-{\\text{i}}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+1+1 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3c080a6ff42aca4bb5dbba2287e3fb4ebe009721ba3a2f1ef790fd5c0bca930"} {"question" "q-28215" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6abedbd615512b719ea8cf60c57c1be5709c60e61b06e8ccc841cea1a2337307"} {"question" "q-28215" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3c080a6ff42aca4bb5dbba2287e3fb4ebe009721ba3a2f1ef790fd5c0bca930"} {"question" "q-28215" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{x}^{2}+2=0"] ["math" "{x}^{2}+2x+2=0"] ["math" "{x}^{2}+x-2=0"] ["math" "{x}^{2}-2x=0"] ["math" "{x}^{2}-x+1=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3c080a6ff42aca4bb5dbba2287e3fb4ebe009721ba3a2f1ef790fd5c0bca930"} {"question" "q-28215" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{x}^{2}+2x+2=0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3c080a6ff42aca4bb5dbba2287e3fb4ebe009721ba3a2f1ef790fd5c0bca930"} {"question" "q-16938" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=\\sqrt{3}+\\text{i}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $2$, find the equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3c080a6ff42aca4bb5dbba2287e3fb4ebe009721ba3a2f1ef790fd5c0bca930"} {"question" "q-16938" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and $x=\\sqrt{3}+\\text{i}$ is a root, its conjugate $x=\\sqrt{3}-\\text{i}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-(\\sqrt{3}+\\text{i}))(x-(\\sqrt{3}-\\text{i}))=0\n$$\n\nBy expanding the parentheses, we can write the equation in standard form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-x(\\sqrt{3}+\\text{i})-x(\\sqrt{3}-\\text{i})+(\\sqrt{3}-\\text{i})(\\sqrt{3}+\\text{i}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-x\\sqrt{3}-x\\text{i}-x\\sqrt{3}+x\\text{i}+3+\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}-{\\text{i}}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\sqrt{3}x+3+1 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\sqrt{3}x+4 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=\\sqrt{3}\\pm \\text{i},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $2.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({x}^{2}-2\\sqrt{3}x+4) & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-4\\sqrt{3}x+8 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3c080a6ff42aca4bb5dbba2287e3fb4ebe009721ba3a2f1ef790fd5c0bca930"} {"question" "q-16938" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6abedbd615512b719ea8cf60c57c1be5709c60e61b06e8ccc841cea1a2337307"} {"question" "q-16938" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3c080a6ff42aca4bb5dbba2287e3fb4ebe009721ba3a2f1ef790fd5c0bca930"} {"question" "q-16938" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2{x}^{2}-2\\sqrt{3}x+8=0"] ["math" "2{x}^{2}-4\\sqrt{3}x+16=0"] ["math" "2{x}^{2}-4\\sqrt{3}x+8=0"] ["math" "2{x}^{2}-6\\sqrt{3}x+18=0"] ["math" "2{x}^{2}-8\\sqrt{3}x+10=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3c080a6ff42aca4bb5dbba2287e3fb4ebe009721ba3a2f1ef790fd5c0bca930"} {"question" "q-16938" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2{x}^{2}-4\\sqrt{3}x+8=0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3c080a6ff42aca4bb5dbba2287e3fb4ebe009721ba3a2f1ef790fd5c0bca930"} {"question" "q-16935" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=\\frac{1}{2}+\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $4$, find the equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3c080a6ff42aca4bb5dbba2287e3fb4ebe009721ba3a2f1ef790fd5c0bca930"} {"question" "q-16935" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and $x=\\frac{1}{2}+\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}$ is a root, its conjugate $x=\\frac{1}{2}-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-(\\frac{1}{2}+\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}))(x-(\\frac{1}{2}-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}))=0\n$$\n\nBy expanding the parentheses, we can write the equation in standard form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-x(\\frac{1}{2}-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i})-x(\\frac{1}{2}+\\sqrt{3}\\text{i})+(\\frac{1}{2}+\\sqrt{3}\\text{i})(\\frac{1}{2}-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\frac{x}{2}+x\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}-\\frac{x}{2}-x\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}+\\frac{1}{4}-3{\\text{i}}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{4}+3 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-x+\\frac{13}{4} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\text{i},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $4.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $4.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({x}^{2}-x+\\frac{13}{4}) & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-4x+13 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3c080a6ff42aca4bb5dbba2287e3fb4ebe009721ba3a2f1ef790fd5c0bca930"} {"question" "q-16935" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6abedbd615512b719ea8cf60c57c1be5709c60e61b06e8ccc841cea1a2337307"} {"question" "q-16935" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3c080a6ff42aca4bb5dbba2287e3fb4ebe009721ba3a2f1ef790fd5c0bca930"} {"question" "q-16935" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "4{x}^{2}+16x+13=0"] ["math" "4{x}^{2}+4x+13=0"] ["math" "4{x}^{2}-13x+4=0"] ["math" "4{x}^{2}-4x+13=0"] ["math" "4{x}^{2}-8x+16=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3c080a6ff42aca4bb5dbba2287e3fb4ebe009721ba3a2f1ef790fd5c0bca930"} {"question" "q-16935" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4{x}^{2}-4x+13=0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3c080a6ff42aca4bb5dbba2287e3fb4ebe009721ba3a2f1ef790fd5c0bca930"} {"question" "q-16936" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=-3+4\\text{i}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $3$, find the equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3c080a6ff42aca4bb5dbba2287e3fb4ebe009721ba3a2f1ef790fd5c0bca930"} {"question" "q-16936" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and $x=-3+4\\text{i}$ is a root, its conjugate $x=-3-4\\text{i}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-(-3+4\\text{i}))(x-(-3-4\\text{i}))=0\n$$\n\nBy expanding the parentheses, we can write the equation in standard form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-x(-3-4\\text{i})-x(-3+4\\text{i})+(-3+4\\text{i})(-3-4\\text{i}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+3x+4x\\text{i}+3x-4x\\text{i}+9-16{\\text{i}}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+6x+9+16 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+6x+25 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=-3\\pm 4\\text{i},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $3.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $3.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3({x}^{2}+6x+25) & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+18x+75 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3c080a6ff42aca4bb5dbba2287e3fb4ebe009721ba3a2f1ef790fd5c0bca930"} {"question" "q-16936" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6abedbd615512b719ea8cf60c57c1be5709c60e61b06e8ccc841cea1a2337307"} {"question" "q-16936" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3c080a6ff42aca4bb5dbba2287e3fb4ebe009721ba3a2f1ef790fd5c0bca930"} {"question" "q-16936" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3{x}^{2}+18x+75=0"] ["math" "3{x}^{2}+25x+6=0"] ["math" "3{x}^{2}+6x+25=0"] ["math" "3{x}^{2}+75x+18=0"] ["math" "3{x}^{2}-18x-25=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3c080a6ff42aca4bb5dbba2287e3fb4ebe009721ba3a2f1ef790fd5c0bca930"} {"question" "q-16936" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3{x}^{2}+18x+75=0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/state.json" "file_sha256" "b3c080a6ff42aca4bb5dbba2287e3fb4ebe009721ba3a2f1ef790fd5c0bca930"} {"question" "e-5776" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A quadratic equation with real coefficients has a root $x=5+4\\textrm{i}.$ What is the other root?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/example-5776.json" "file_sha256" "a0233adccd24afaad1cb010cbbb81732a7df65c28be70db050078a3676d038f4"} {"question" "e-5776" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given that the quadratic equation has real coefficients and that $x=5+4\\textrm{i}$ is a root. Therefore, its complex conjugate $x=5-4\\textrm{i}$ is also a root." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/example-5776.json" "file_sha256" "a0233adccd24afaad1cb010cbbb81732a7df65c28be70db050078a3676d038f4"} {"question" "e-5777" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=-3\\textrm{i}.$ Given that the coefficient of $x^2$ in the equation is $2,$ find the equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/example-5777.json" "file_sha256" "8e016a862e7482ab784642dc1c320b6fcbea8abb997e6a1f3d6541f25e6094f6"} {"question" "e-5777" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and  $x=-3\\textrm{i}$ is a root, its conjugate  $x = 3\\textrm{i}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-3\\textrm{i})(x+3\\textrm{i})= 0\n$$\n\nMultiplying out the parentheses and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-(3\\text{i})^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-9(-1) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+9 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=\\pm 3\\textrm{i},$ but the coefficient of $x^2$ is not $2.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({x}^{2}+9) & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+18 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/example-5777.json" "file_sha256" "8e016a862e7482ab784642dc1c320b6fcbea8abb997e6a1f3d6541f25e6094f6"} {"question" "e-5778" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=6-8\\textrm{i}.$ Given that the coefficient of $x^2$ in the equation is $-1,$ find the equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/example-5778.json" "file_sha256" "4a1f933110cd99d57cb4f9879308330fd441c876112152a5387def33b5f37594"} {"question" "e-5778" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and $x=6-8\\textrm{i}$ is a root, its conjugate $x=6+8\\textrm{i}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n\\Bigl(x-(6-8\\textrm{i})\\Bigr)\\Bigl(x-(6+8\\textrm{i})\\Bigr) = 0\n$$\n\nBy expanding the parentheses, we can write the equation in standard form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-x(6+8\\text{i})-x(6-8\\text{i})+(6-8\\text{i})(6+8\\text{i}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-6x-8x\\text{i}-6x+8x\\text{i}+36-64{\\text{i}}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-12x+36+64 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-12x+100 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x= 6 \\pm 8\\textrm{i},$ but the coefficient of $x^2$ is not $-1.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $-1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({x}^{2}-12x+100) & =0 \\\\ -{x}^{2}+12x-100 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977623/example-5778.json" "file_sha256" "4a1f933110cd99d57cb4f9879308330fd441c876112152a5387def33b5f37594"}] "usage" {"e-5777" [] "q-28215" [] "e-5776" [] "q-28523" [] "q-28502" [] "e-5778" []} "basis" 810 "retractions" [[17592186115609 :question/problem "A quadratic equation with real coefficients has a root $x = 3 - \\textrm{i}$. What is the other root?"] [17592186115612 :question/problem "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x = 2\\textrm{i}$. Given that the coefficient of $x^{2}$ in the equation is $3$, find the equation."] [17592186115588 :question/problem "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x =-1 + \\textrm{i}$. Given that the coefficient of $x^{2}$ in the equation is $1$, find the equation."] [17592186115024 :question/problem "A quadratic equation with real coefficients has a root $x=5+4\\textrm{i}$. What is the other root?"] [17592186115024 :question/worked-solution "We're given that the quadratic equation has real coefficients and that\n\n$$\nx=5+4\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nis a root. Therefore, its complex conjugate\n\n$$\nx=5-4\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nis also a root."] [17592186115025 :question/problem "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=-3\\textrm{i}$. Given that the coefficient of $x^2$ in the equation is $2$, find the equation."] [17592186115025 :question/worked-solution "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and\n\n$$\nx=-3\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nis a root, its conjugate\n\n$$\nx = 3\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nmust also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-3\\textrm{i})(x+3\\textrm{i})= 0\n$$\n\nMultiplying out the parentheses and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - (3\\textrm{i})^{2} &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} - 9(-1) &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} + 9 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots\n\n$$\nx=\\pm 3\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nbut the coefficient of $x^2$ is not $2$. Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $2$. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2(x^{2} + 9) &= 0 \\\\\n2x^{2} + 18 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186115026 :question/problem "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=6-8\\textrm{i}$. Given that the coefficient of $x^2$ in the equation is $-1$, find the equation."] [17592186115026 :question/worked-solution "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and\n\n$$\nx=6-8\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nis a root, its conjugate\n\n$$\nx=6+8\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nmust also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n\\Bigl(x-(6-8\\textrm{i})\\Bigr)\\Bigl(x-(6+8\\textrm{i})\\Bigr) = 0\n$$\n\nBy expanding the parentheses, we can write the equation in standard form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - x(6 + 8\\textrm{i}) - x(6 - 8\\textrm{i}) + (6 - 8\\textrm{i})(6 + 8\\textrm{i}) &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} - 6x - 8x\\textrm{i} - 6x + 8x\\textrm{i} + 36 - 64\\textrm{i}^{2} &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} - 12x + 36 + 64 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} - 12x + 100 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots\n\n$$\nx= 6 \\pm 8\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nbut the coefficient of $x^2$ is not $-1$. Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $-1$. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n- (x^{2} - 12x + 100) &= 0 \\\\\n- x^{2} + 12x - 100 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186112929 17592186112930 17592186112931 17592186112932 17592186112933 17592186113055 17592186113056 17592186113057 17592186113058 17592186113059 17592186113073 17592186113074 17592186113075 17592186113076 17592186113077] "immutable_fields" [[17592186112928 :answer-field/correct] [17592186112928 :answer-field/id] [17592186112928 :answer-field/key] [17592186112928 :answer-field/type] [17592186113054 :answer-field/correct] [17592186113054 :answer-field/id] [17592186113054 :answer-field/key] [17592186113054 :answer-field/type] [17592186113072 :answer-field/correct] [17592186113072 :answer-field/id] [17592186113072 :answer-field/key] [17592186113072 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
